DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ccii program is used to check in new revisions to RCS files.

     When a file is checked in, it is stored in the RCS directory with a spe‐
     cific revision number, and the original file itself is deleted.  The RCS
     file is stored with the same name, but with ‘,v’ appended.  ccii will
     prompt for a check-in message, to be stored with the file, which can be
     displayed using rlog(1).

     Revision numbering starts at 1.1 and increases logically.  Numbering can
     be altered using the --kk option, however.  The ‘,v’ suffix can also be al‐
     tered, using the --xx option.

     ccii also supports keyword substitution – see the rcs(1) man page for more
     information.

     The following options are supported:

     --dd[_d_a_t_e]
             Uses _d_a_t_e for check-in date and time.  If _d_a_t_e is not specified,
             use the working file's last modification time.

     --ff[_r_e_v]
             Force check-in – even if no changes have been made to the working
             file.

     --II[_r_e_v]
             Interactive mode.

     --ii[_r_e_v]
             Only do initial check-in.  Print error and refuse to do check-in
             if the RCS file already exists.

     --jj[_r_e_v]
             Only do update check-in.  Print error and refuse to do check-in
             if the RCS file does not already exist.

     --kk[_r_e_v]
             Search the working file for keywords and set the revision number,
             creation date, state and author to the values found in these key‐
             words instead of computing them.

     --ll[_r_e_v]
             The same as --rr, but also immediately checks out the deposited re‐
             vision and locks it.  This is useful if you wish to continue to
             edit the working file after check-in.

     --MM[_r_e_v]
             Set the modification time of the file to the date of the re‐
             trieved revision.

     --mm_m_s_g   Specify a log message.  A line beginning with a hash character
             (‘#’) is considered a comment and ignored.

     --NN_s_y_m_b_o_l
             Assign the symbolic name _s_y_m_b_o_l to the checked in revision, over‐
             writing any previous assignment of _s_y_m_b_o_l.

     --nn_s_y_m_b_o_l
             The same as --NN except it does not overwrite previous symbols.

     --qq      Be quiet about reporting.

     --rr[_r_e_v]
             Check in revision _r_e_v.  However, if _r_e_v is not specified the
             meaning is completely different – override any --ll or --uu options,
             ensuring the default behaviour of releasing a lock and removing
             the working file.

     --ss_s_t_a_t_e
             Sets the state of the deposited revision to the identifier _s_t_a_t_e.
             The specified value may not contain a space character.

     --tt[_s_t_r]
             Change the descriptive text.  The argument _s_t_r is interpreted as
             the name of a file containing the descriptive text or, if pre‐
             fixed with a ‘-’, the actual descriptive text itself.  If no ar‐
             gument is given, this option is ignored (for compatibility rea‐
             sons).

     --uu[_r_e_v]
             The same as --rr, but also immediately checks out the deposited re‐
             vision read-only.  Useful if you wish to read the working file
             after check-in.

     --VV      Print RCS's version number.

     --ww_u_s_e_r_n_a_m_e
             Uses _u_s_e_r_n_a_m_e as the author field of the deposited revision.

     --xx_s_u_f_f_i_x_e_s
             Specifies the suffixes for RCS files.  Suffixes should be sepa‐
             rated by the ‘/’ character.

     --zz_t_z    Specify the date output format in keyword substitution, and the
             default time zone for _d_a_t_e used in the --dd option.

EENNVVIIRROONNMMEENNTT
     RCSINIT  If set, this variable should contain a list of space-delimited
              options that are prepended to the argument list.

     TMPDIR   When set, this variable specifies the directory where temporary
              files are to be created.  The default is set to _/_t_m_p.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The ccii utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

